		{
			"type": "text",
			"version": 919,
			"versionNonce": 1134183559,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"id": "ayPCFbcS",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"angle": 0,
			"x": 475.02541612932384,
			"y": -252.2249529046113,
			"strokeColor": "#c92a2a",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"width": 351,
			"height": 350,
			"seed": 436082966,
			"groupIds": [],
			"roundness": null,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1675185608875,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 1,
			"text": "Cortisol triggers brain stem to \nincrease activation of amygdala so\nit is more vigilant / alert to \ndanger. SNS/HPAA system \nincreased.\n\nRelease of cortisol and \nnorepinephrine suppresses activity in\nthe hippocampus and prefrontal \ncortex,  which usually inhibits \namygdala. Amygdala is now let off \nthe leash to act uninhibited.\nThe cycle continues with greater \nfuel for the fire.",
			"rawText": "Cortisol triggers brain stem to increase activation of amygdala so it is more vigilant / alert to danger. SNS/HPAA system increased.\n\nRelease of cortisol and norepinephrine suppresses activity in the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex,  which usually inhibits amygdala. Amygdala is now let off the leash to act uninhibited.\nThe cycle continues with greater fuel for the fire.",
			"baseline": 343,
			"textAlign": "center",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "IJF6XkT6sheFMhWs3gINK",
			"originalText": "Cortisol triggers brain stem to increase activation of amygdala so it is more vigilant / alert to danger. SNS/HPAA system increased.\n\nRelease of cortisol and norepinephrine suppresses activity in the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex,  which usually inhibits amygdala. Amygdala is now let off the leash to act uninhibited.\nThe cycle continues with greater fuel for the fire."
		},
